/-!
# Pointwise Koszul regularity after extension of the ground field

Source: Stacks Project, Descent, Tag 0694, Koszul-regular clause, and More on Morphisms,
Tag 06B8, residue-field descent; openness is Tag 0383
(blueprint: lem-sfb-field-extension-immersion-koszul-points).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- Koszul regularity near a chosen lifted source point is equivalent to Koszul
regularity near its image, for an immersion into a scheme locally of finite type
over a field and an arbitrary extension of that field.

Source: Stacks Project, Descent, Tag 0694, pointwise consequence used in Tag 06B8
(blueprint: lem-sfb-field-extension-immersion-koszul-points).

Proof plan, following the seven steps of the DAG node:
1. Factor the immersion through an open target, then choose an affine chart `Spec A`
   around the image point. Its closed subscheme is `Spec (A/I)`. Intersect source and
   target neighbourhoods to identify their restricted ideals with this same ideal.
   Use the affine-open basis and `quotientBaseChange_closedImmersion_ideal`.
2. The projection after field extension is flat (field-extension flatness and flat
   base change). Flat ascent of Koszul-regular immersions on the chosen open source
   and target neighbourhoods proves the forward implication. Open immersions remain
   open under base change.
3. Conversely, choose an affine regular-equation neighbourhood `Spec B` upstairs.
   The affine Koszul-regularity criterion and quotient base change identify its
   ideal with `IB`, generated by a finite Koszul-regular sequence.
4. The projection is open by universal openness over a field. Choose a principal
   neighbourhood `D(h)` of the downstairs point contained in the image of `Spec B`.
   The basic-open and localization identifications give rings `A_h` and `B_b`,
   where `b` is the image of `h`.
5. Every point of `D(h)` lifts into `Spec B`, hence into `D(b)`. Thus the restricted
   affine map is surjective and flat, by the affine flatness criterion. The flat and
   surjective-spectrum criterion makes `A_h → B_b` faithfully flat.
6. Localization is flat; `RingTheory.Sequence.isKoszulRegular_of_flat` preserves
   the upstairs generators in `B_b`. They generate the extension of `I A_h`.
   Faithfully flat descent of locally Koszul-regular ideals, with upstairs localizer
   equal to one, supplies regular generators on a further principal neighbourhood
   of the specified downstairs prime.
7. Quotient base change identifies the ideal on that neighbourhood with the original
   immersion ideal. The affine criterion proves downstairs Koszul regularity.
   Empty sequences are allowed throughout; an empty base-changed source has no point
   to check. No finite-degree or separability condition is used. -/
theorem isKoszulRegularImmersionNear_fieldExtension_iff
    {k K : Type u} [Field k] [Field K] [Algebra k K]
    (Z P : Over (Spec (CommRingCat.of k))) (i : Z ⟶ P)
    [LocallyOfFiniteType P.hom] [IsImmersion i.left]
    (zK : ((Over.pullback (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k K)))).obj Z).left) :
    IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear
        ((Over.pullback (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k K)))).map i).left zK ↔
      IsKoszulRegularImmersionNear i.left
        ((pullback.fst Z.hom (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap k K)))) zK) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
